Question: If you assign a value type to an object (e.g., object obj = 50;),
what is this process called in C#?

This process is called "boxing" in C#. When you assign a value type (like an int) to an object, 
the value type is wrapped inside an object, allowing it to be treated as a reference type.

What happens if you then try to do int x = obj; without casting?

If you try to assign the object back to an int without casting (e.g., int x = obj;), you will get a compile-time error.
So, you need to explicitly cast the object back to the value type (e.g., int x = (int)obj;) to retrieve the original value.
And this process is called "unboxing".
